zcode packaging notes
=====================

What is built
-------------
Only the CLI (apps/zcode-cli) and its terminal UI. The desktop app, web UI
and server are excluded from the source tarball (_service `exclude`s). No
official plugin ships: most (zcode-guide, image-search, ...) have no source
in the public repository, and browser-use and node-repl-host, which do, are
excluded with the browser automation they exist for. So browser/computer use
and `--browser-use=headless` (playwright-core) are unavailable. Dynamic
workflows and `/workflow` work in yolo mode only, not in plan mode; in build
and accept-edits mode every EvalWorkflowSnippet asks, and so does resuming a
run the user stopped. No .env file is read (zcode-no-dotenv.patch):
configuration and API keys come from the environment. PAC proxy files do not
work, as upstream: the CJS bundle cannot locate quickjs-wasi's wasm. On
start the CLI fetches its built-in provider list from zcode.z.ai, as
upstream does. Upstream builds with pnpm; here the CLI's 17 workspace
packages plus the apps/zcode-cli root are an npm workspace
(zcode-npm-workspace.json), vendored through the node_modules service.

Patches
-------
zcode-contracts-zod-v3.patch        npm resolves contracts' zod ^3.24 against
                                    the hoisted zod 4.6.5; importing the zod 3
                                    API as zod/v3 builds against either. The
                                    contracts schemas were verified
                                    byte-identical.
zcode-tui-distro-opentui.patch      @mbears/opentui-* 0.2.15 (a fork with an
                                    unpublished native library) -> upstream
                                    @opentui/* at the distribution opentui
                                    version.
zcode-no-cwd-plugins.patch          upstream also looks for official plugins
                                    under the working directory; a build that
                                    does not ship them (this one) loads a
                                    planted one. %check guards it.
zcode-adapters-no-koffi.patch       koffi is Windows-only; dropping it keeps
                                    its prebuilt .node files out of the
                                    vendored sources.
zcode-js-text-search.patch          the ripgrep (WASI) worker imports
                                    "ripgrep" from the working directory;
                                    search with the JavaScript engine, which
                                    was the fallback in use anyway.
zcode-no-dotenv.patch               the CLI loaded the nearest .env above the
                                    working directory into the environment
                                    every tool process inherits (BASH_ENV,
                                    LD_PRELOAD, GIT_CONFIG_*, search
                                    commands); it reads none now.
zcode-project-config-trust.patch    zcode.json / .zcode/config.json from the
                                    working directory upward: project MCP
                                    servers are not auto-started (upstream's
                                    untrusted path, /mcp connect), and
                                    permission, plugins, storage and network
                                    are ignored there. %check plants both.
zcode-workflow-snippet-ask.patch    snippets without world.run ran unasked,
                                    contained only by node:vm; all ask now.
zcode-git-snapshot-safe.patch       the first-prompt git snapshot, the TUI
                                    branch lookup and workflow git reads pass
                                    -c safe.bareRepository=explicit
                                    -c core.fsmonitor=false
                                    -c log.showSignature=false.
zcode-write-approval-confined.patch the drafts pre-approval, accept-edits
                                    mode and the agent-memory rule compared
                                    path strings only; they now also need
                                    the target inside the workspace root
                                    (Bash moves the working directory;
                                    agent memory: inside its memory root),
                                    outside .git in any letter case, no
                                    symlink on the way, and a project or
                                    local agent memory root behind a
                                    symlink is not used.
zcode-project-commands-no-shell.patch
                                    project-scope commands (including
                                    configured extra command roots, which
                                    upstream tags project) no longer run
                                    !`...` blocks; user commands still do.
zcode-workflow-amend-ask.patch      the owner rule ran any AmendWorkflow of
                                    a run this session started unasked, a
                                    new script included; now only retunes
                                    and amendments keeping the script.
zcode-bash-always-ask.patch         Bash asks in build, accept-edits and
                                    plan mode (entered by the model
                                    unasked) whatever the read-only
                                    classifier says; it passed sed e/w/W
                                    and s///e,w, uniq's output operand and
                                    bfs -rm. Saved rules and
                                    permission.allowedTools still apply
                                    outside plan mode, as upstream.
zcode-explore-no-yolo.patch         the built-in Explore agent ran in yolo
                                    whatever the session's mode; it follows
                                    the session now.
zcode-workflows-yolo-only.patch     the TUI and -p answered CreateWorkflow
                                    and AmendWorkflow with allow in every
                                    mode, and world.run runs any command
                                    unchecked; both now allow them in yolo
                                    mode only and deny them otherwise, core
                                    denies them in plan mode (entered from
                                    yolo too) and applies the owner rule in
                                    yolo mode only.
zcode-workflow-resume-ask.patch     ResumeWorkflowRun needed no approval;
                                    resuming any stopped run (also one
                                    interrupted by quitting) asks outside
                                    yolo mode.
zcode-workflow-private-tmpdir.patch the run-entry file fell back to the
                                    fixed $TMPDIR/zcode-workflow-runs,
                                    which another user can plant; now a new
                                    mkdtemp directory (0700).
patches/@ai-sdk__*.patch            upstream's own pnpm patches, applied by
                                    hand in %build (npm ignores
                                    patchedDependencies).

Reported upstream as GHSA-3c25-29h8-g8pq (private vulnerability report,
2026-09-30): the working-directory plugin lookup, the ripgrep worker, .env
loading, project-config trust, snippet approval, the git snapshot, write
auto-approval through symlinks, the agent-memory root symlink, project
command shell expansion, the read-only Bash classifier (sed e/w/W and
s///e,w, uniq's output operand, bfs -rm), EnterPlanMode without a prompt,
Explore in yolo, the memory agent's classifier Bash, allow rules skipping
read-only sub-commands, the CLI clients approving CreateWorkflow and
AmendWorkflow unasked, project saved workflows shadowing global ones, the
AmendWorkflow owner rule, ResumeWorkflowRun, the shared
$TMPDIR/zcode-workflow-runs fallback and the project-id slug collisions.
Not reported: upstream has issues disabled and takes no outside pull
requests, so zod/v3 and the TUI quitting on the first Ctrl-C (the startup
screen's key handler stays mounted; root.unmount() before the app render
fixes it) stay downstream notes.

Residuals, accepted (other writes ask):
- a .git/config that arrives inside an archive (not a clone) is untrusted
  for every git command zcode or the model runs: fsmonitor, filter.*.clean,
  diff.external and textconv all come from it;
- the memory-extraction agent runs in yolo and runs the Bash its read-only
  classifier accepts (memory-agent-loop.ts), the classifier
  zcode-bash-always-ask.patch stops trusting elsewhere;
- a saved Bash allow rule also covers read-only sub-commands it does not
  name (make:* allows "make && <read-only command>");
- workflow actors run in yolo: a run starts only from a yolo session and
  resumes outside it only when the user approves; a run in flight keeps
  running after the session leaves yolo; the user's own /dwf resume runs
  unasked; /expert switches the session to yolo (/workflow does not);
- projects whose ids collide as slugs share the saved permission mode and
  always-allow rules;
- draft and run-entry files (fixed names) are written through a
  .zcode/workflow-drafts or .zcode/workflow-runs symlink the repository
  planted (LOW).
`zcode -p` without --mode runs in yolo (upstream's headless default); pass
--mode build to keep the asks (Bash and workflows are then refused: nobody
can answer, and workflows need yolo).

Project config, as patched: MCP servers are listed as untrusted; /mcp list
does not show their command, so read zcode.json before /mcp connect.
`zcode plugins ... --scope project|workspace` writes settings this build
ignores. features, memory, logging, skills and ui still come from project
config (none of them adds code execution; memory is on by default).

Lockstep
--------
- The TUI binds libopentui.so over a private, unversioned FFI ABI:
  @opentui/core and @opentui/react in the patched tui/package.json and
  %opentui_version must agree (%prep fails otherwise); the opentui package
  is only required to be at least that release. A newer libopentui can
  differ (0.5.10 -> 0.5.11 changed 19 signatures), so re-vendor zcode when
  opentui moves; %check's resolveRenderLib load catches a missing symbol at
  build time only. opentui itself follows opencode's pin. On Node it needs
  node:ffi, i.e. Node >= 26.4 with --experimental-ffi (the launcher passes
  it; --allow-ffi would need --permission).
- esbuild: the npm override, %esbuild_version and Factory's esbuild must be
  the same release (the npm API refuses any other binary). A Factory esbuild
  bump makes this package unresolvable until the three are moved together.
- %cli_version must match apps/zcode-cli/package.json (%prep checks it).

Dependency versions
-------------------
npm resolves the newest release inside upstream's declared ranges, not the
versions in upstream's pnpm-lock.yaml: that lock is older, and pinning to it
brought back releases with published advisories (protobufjs, undici,
basic-ftp, ip-address, shell-quote). Overrides are kept to what the build
needs (react as upstream, the two @ai-sdk packages upstream patches,
esbuild) plus security lifts above upstream's exact pins:
@ai-sdk/provider-utils 4.0.33 (CVE-2026-8769; scoped under @ai-sdk/anthropic
and ai), @opentelemetry/core 2.8.0 (CVE-2026-54285) and ws 8.21.0
(CVE-2026-45736, CVE-2026-48779; via the TUI's own pin in %prep). Unused
path: @ai-sdk/openai's downloadBlob loads undici, which is not in the
runtime tree; only OpenAI image edits from URLs reach it and zcode does not
use them. The vendored set must stay free of advisories: run an OSV batch
query (https://api.osv.dev/v1/querybatch) over every lock entry on each bump
and lift what it finds to the first fixed release.

Bumping
-------
1. Set the tag in _service; run the obs_scm, tar, recompress and set_version
   services; `file` the unpacked tree (no prebuilt binaries expected).
2. Refresh the source patches; check the tui pins and the pnpm
   patchedDependencies versions against the overrides.
3. Regenerate package-lock.json from a tree prepared exactly as %prep does,
   with `npm install --package-lock-only --ignore-scripts`; run the OSV
   sweep. Then delete every lock entry that is optional and carries
   os/cpu/libc (platform binaries); `source-map` is the one pure-JS optional
   dependency, so it is a root devDependency.
4. Run the node_modules service. It needs obs-service-node_modules >= 3.0
   (older versions reject workspace entries) with legacy-container.
5. Re-check the build order (`turbo run build --filter=@zcode/cli^... --dry`
   in an upstream checkout) and re-derive License over the non-dev lock
   entries.

local-npm-registry installs with --no-package-lock: the lock decides which
tarballs are vendored, and the vendored set then decides the tree.

Prebuilt artefacts
------------------
Shipped: web-tree-sitter's runtime (tree-sitter.wasm) and the oniguruma
regex engine shiki inlines as base64 in @shikijs/engine-oniguruma, both C
compiled to wasm; %install asserts that no other wasm and no ELF/PE/Mach-O
file ships (checked by content). Vendored only (source RPM): quickjs-wasi's
wasm modules (named *.so; PAC resolver), the opentui grammar wasm (replaced
by links to tree-sitter-*-wasm), shiki's standalone onig.wasm,
web-tree-sitter's lib/ and debug/ copies (removed in %install), and the
base64-inlined wasm in tsx (es-module-lexer; a build tool, needed to stage
the provider config) and undici (llhttp).
